course: world
lesson: 52
title: Simple machines and how they help
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A simple machine is a tool that makes a job easier by changing how much force we need or which way we push. The lever, the wheel and axle, the pulley and the ramp are simple machines.
explain: A lever is a stiff bar that turns around a point called the pivot. If you push far from the pivot, a small force can lift a heavy load that sits close to the pivot — that is how a long crowbar lifts a big stone.
explain: Machines do not give us free work: to use less force we must push over a longer distance. Walking up a long gentle ramp is easier than climbing a steep ladder, but the path is longer.
explain: A wheel and axle is a big wheel fixed to a thin rod; turning the big wheel a long way turns the axle with a strong force, as in a door knob or a steering wheel. Wheels also roll, which makes much less friction than dragging.
explain: A pulley is a wheel with a rope over it. One fixed pulley changes the direction of the force, so you can pull down to lift up; several pulleys together let a small force lift a heavy load with a longer pull of rope.
fact: A simple machine makes work easier by changing the size or the direction of a force.
fact: A lever is a bar that turns around a pivot.
fact: The further from the pivot you push, the less force you need to lift a load.
fact: A see-saw, scissors, a bottle opener and a wheelbarrow all use levers.
fact: A ramp lets you lift a load with less force by moving it along a longer path.
fact: A wheel and axle turns a small force on a big wheel into a stronger turn on the thin axle.
fact: Rolling on wheels makes much less friction than dragging along the ground.
fact: A single fixed pulley changes the direction of the pull but not its size.
fact: Several pulleys together let you lift a heavy load with a smaller force but a longer pull of rope.
fact: A machine that saves force always makes you move further; it never saves work for free.
exercise: What does a simple machine do? | Makes a job easier by changing the force we need | Creates energy out of nothing | Makes objects lighter than they are | Removes gravity from the load
exercise: What is the point a lever turns around called? | The pivot | The axle | The ramp | The handle
exercise: Where should you push on a crowbar to lift a heavy stone most easily? | At the far end from the pivot | Right up close, next to the pivot | Exactly on top of the stone itself | In the very middle of the long bar
exercise: Why is it easier to push a box up a long ramp than to lift it straight up? | You use less force but move it a longer way | The ramp makes the box lighter | The ramp removes all of the friction completely | Gravity does not act on a ramp
exercise: How does a single pulley at the top of a flagpole help? | You pull down to raise the flag | It makes the flag weigh much less | It doubles the strength of your arms | It lets the flag rise without any force
exercise: Which of these is a lever? | A see-saw | A wheel on a toy car | A long sloping path | A flagpole rope
exercise: Why does a door knob make a door easier to open than turning the thin rod inside it? | The big knob turns the thin rod with a stronger force | The knob makes the door lighter | The knob removes the friction of the hinge | The knob pulls the door by magnetism
exercise: Why do we put heavy suitcases on wheels? | Rolling makes much less friction than dragging | Wheels make the suitcase lighter | Wheels cancel the pull of gravity | Wheels push the suitcase along all by themselves
exercise: What do we pay for when a machine lets us use less force? | We must move a longer distance | We must use more force later | The load becomes heavier | The machine becomes hotter than fire
exercise: Why do roads up steep mountains zigzag? | A longer, gentler slope needs less force to climb | Zigzags make the mountain lower | Straight roads are always more slippery | Zigzags remove all the friction from the wheels of cars
exercise: Which simple machine is a wheelbarrow mostly? | A lever with the wheel as the pivot | A pulley with a rope running over the top | A ramp turned over on its side for loads | Only a wheel and nothing else at all
exercise: How does a set of several pulleys help builders? | A small pull lifts a heavy load, with more rope pulled | It makes the load disappear for a moment | It lets the load lift itself up | It makes the rope pull much faster with no effort
exercise: Why are scissors with long handles better for cutting thick card? | Your hands push further from the pivot, so they press harder | Long handles make the blades sharper | Long handles make the card softer | Long handles remove all friction
test: A girl and her much heavier father sit on a see-saw. How can they balance? | The father sits closer to the middle | The father sits right at the very end of the board | The girl sits closer to the middle | They can never balance
test: Which is true about every simple machine? | It may save force, but you must move further | It always produces more work than you put into it | It makes loads lose their weight | It works without anyone pushing or pulling
test: A worker must get a heavy barrel onto a lorry. Which helps most without a crane? | Rolling it up a long plank as a ramp | Lifting it with a much shorter plank | Dragging it along the rough gravel first | Pushing it down from the top of the lorry
test: Why is a long spanner better than a short one for undoing a stiff nut? | You push further from the nut, so you need less force | A long spanner is made of stronger metal | A long spanner makes the nut smaller | A long spanner removes friction from the nut
test: A pulley is fixed at the top of a well. What is the main help it gives? | You can pull down on the rope to raise the bucket | The bucket becomes half as heavy | The water becomes lighter to lift | The rope lifts the full bucket up the well all by itself
test: A ramp is made longer and less steep. What happens to the push needed? | Smaller, but over a longer path | It becomes bigger and the path is shorter | It stays exactly the same as before | It becomes zero, with no push needed at all
test: Why does a steering wheel have a large rim instead of a small one? | A big wheel turns the thin shaft with a stronger force | A big wheel makes the car go faster | A big wheel makes the car lighter | A big wheel keeps the car on the road
test: If you move the pivot of a lever closer to the heavy load, what happens? | Lifting the load needs less force | Lifting the load needs more force | The load becomes heavier | Nothing changes at all
test: why push a heavy box up a ramp not just lift it | The ramp needs less force but a longer path | The ramp makes the box lighter | The ramp saves both force and distance at the same time | The ramp pulls the box up itself
test: door handle far from the hinge, why tho | Pushing further from the pivot needs less force | It looks nicer on the door | It stops the door from closing | The hinge would break if the handle were any nearer
test: my mate says a pulley always makes lifting lighter. true? | No, one fixed pulley only changes the direction of the pull | Yes, any pulley halves the weight | Yes, pulleys make loads lighter | No, a pulley makes lifting heavier
test: a video said a clever machine can give you more work than you put in. right? | No, a machine that saves force makes you move further | Yes, levers make free work | Yes, if the machine is big enough | No, machines always waste every bit of the work you put in
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of a simple machine? | It alters the magnitude or the direction of an applied force | It produces a greater quantity of work than is supplied to it | It eliminates the necessity of any applied force whatsoever | It diminishes the mass of the load to be displaced
exam: It is given that a load is raised by means of a lever. Ascertain the condition under which the least effort shall be required. | The effort is applied at the greatest distance from the pivot | The effort is applied immediately adjacent to the pivot | The load is positioned at the greatest distance from the pivot | The pivot is situated at the extremity of the bar
exam: Identify the principal advantage conferred by an inclined plane in respect of the elevation of a heavy load. | A lesser force suffices, the load being conveyed along a longer path | The load is elevated through a shorter path with greater force | The weight of the load is permanently diminished | The friction upon the load is entirely abolished
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning a single fixed pulley. | It alters the direction of the pull but not its magnitude | It reduces the required pull to one half of the load | It increases the required pull to twice the load | It alters neither the direction nor the magnitude of the pull
